From c2b9b38478f8a3371059ec6006f11c4849b15384 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 07:44:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: use a transport mode for native streams, never at
 the cadence's cost

Whether a stream plays natively was decided in the output mode current
at each decision point: when VideoPlayer chose the refresh rate (still
the GUI mode), when the codec was opened, when RenderManager configured
the first picture, and in the renderer. The mode selection itself never
looked for a 3840x2160 mode, so a stream whose own mode is 1080p (a
1080p Dolby Vision stream with a whitelist that contains 1080p modes,
or any stream with a 1080p GUI and the adjustment off) lost native
output, and a stream opened before its mode switch (no frame rate in
the container) was decoded without its enhancement layer.

- The stream-level decisions (dual-layer decoding, the forced FFmpeg
  decoder, the mode selection) now ask DVBRIDGE::WantsNativeOutputMode:
  native output in a transport mode, whatever the current mode. The
  renderer still asks WantsNativeOutput, in the current mode; that
  verdict is the one logged and shown in the player information.
- ChooseBestResolution() makes Kodi's usual choice first, exactly the
  one for any other stream: the exact 25, 29.97 and 30 Hz modes that
  kodi-9990 added to the default whitelist for native streams are gone
  (a 25 fps stream gets 2160p50, as in HDR10; a user whitelist with
  2160p25 still gets that mode). Only then, for a native stream, a mode
  that is not a transport mode is replaced by the 3840x2160 transport
  mode with the same refresh rate, among the modes the whitelist
  allows. The cadence is never changed: without such a mode Kodi's
  choice stays and the stream plays without native output.
- The decision holds for the whole stream: VideoPlayer sets it when the
  stream opens, and pictures only add to it. RenderManager chose again
  from every reconfiguring picture, so a stream that moved to the
  ordinary output (its RPUs missing), or whose first picture had no
  RPU, could switch the display mode during playback.
- With the refresh-rate adjustment off, the refresh rate stays as the
  user set it; the size changes to 3840x2160 only if that mode exists at
  the same refresh rate (for example a 1080p60 GUI). A following stream
  without native output in a playlist gets the GUI mode back (Kodi stays
  in the full-screen video window between items), and Kodi returns to
  the GUI mode when playback stops. A 2160p 100/120 Hz GUI keeps its
  refresh rate and the ordinary output.

Streams that are not for native output are unaffected.
